But is this "really" true?  alt.extropians is not a business or organization. 
In implementation and practice, it is nothing more than an online verson of
"let's have a get-together and chat and debate about [what we think is] 
extropian."  If alt.extropians was a business, non-profit org, service pro-
vided by a company, etc, then I think you'd have a case for trademark in-
fringement, but a newsgroup is none of those.  It is a debate.  Anyway,
this will certainly be my last post on this subject, since I am not a 
trademark lawyer, and as you point out this is likely not the best place
for this.  But, if anyone can clarify how alt.extropians (whatever its merits
or lack thereof) infringes ExI's trademarks, please do so by email. Thanks.
I find this to be of some importance, since it is basically an issue of
"is this newsgroup anything other than a discussion?".  If the answer is no,
then crying trademark infringement is not only silly, but an attack on the 1st;
if the answer is yes, and this idea of a newsgroup as some sort of entity 
with specific legal rights and obligations spreads, there could be some BIG 
repercussions in the long run, not only in UseNet, but in any network with
a similar format of online conferences (e.g. FidoNet, RBBSNet, Intellec, etc).

