Dave Burns says:
> "The Michelson-Morley experiment of 1887 , which Einstein mentions in
> support of his theory and which the textbooks have since falsely enshrined
> as the crucial evidence which compelled him to formulate it, actually did
> not give the result required by relartivity! It admittedly substantiated
> its authors' claim that the relative motion of the earth and the 'ether'
> did not exceed a quarter of the earth's orbital velocity. But the actually
> observed effect was not negligible; or has, at any rate, not been proved
> negligible up to this day. The presence of a positive effect in the
> observations of Michelson and Morley was pointed out first by W. M. Hicks
> in 1902 and was later evaluated by D. C. Miller as corresponding to and
> 'ether-drift' of eight to nine kilometres per second."
> 
> >From "Personal Knowledge: Towards a Post-Critical Philosophy," by Michael
> Polanyi, page 12. Any typos are mine.

This sounds like bull. Why?

Because Michaelson and Morley had enormous trouble just getting their
apparatus stable enough to observe anything at all. In the initial
experiments that Michaelson did when in Berlin, he couldn't get the
apparatus to work because nearby vibrations screwed it up too much.
(The apparatus consisted of a marble slab floated in mercury atop
which the device itself -- basically a light source who's output was
put through a beam splitter, thrown through two perpendicular paths,
and reassembled at an interferometer.) The notion that their results
could have been considered error-free enough to indicate an "ether
drift" that small demonstrates that the author you are quoting doesn't
know how to evaluate the difference between experimental error and
real results.

